    <section>
      <h2 id="nature-of-the-project">1. Nature of the project</h2>
      <p>
        AuroFlow is an independent,
        experimental software project for managing
        virtual machines on third-party infrastructure.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is not a commercial cloud computing provider,
        managed hosting service or official Microsoft product.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="third-party-terms-of-service">2. Third-party terms of service</h2>
      <p>
        Using this project does not exempt users from
        the terms, acceptable use policies, quotas or
        technical restrictions imposed by external providers.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/github-terms/github-terms-of-service">
            GitHub Terms of Service
          </a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://ngrok.com/tos">
            ngrok Terms of Service
          </a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://huggingface.co/terms-of-service">
            Hugging Face Terms of Service
          </a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/terms/">
            Cloudflare Terms
          </a>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The technical ability to run a workload does not
        establish that the workload is permitted.
        Users must verify that their intended usage is
        authorized by each relevant provider.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="microsoft-windows-licensing">3. Microsoft Windows licensing</h2>
      <p>
        Microsoft Windows is proprietary software.
        AuroFlow does not grant a Windows
        license or any other Microsoft software entitlement.
      </p>
      <p>
        Operating system activation does not establish
        that a valid license has been obtained.
        Activation tools, including Microsoft Activation
        Scripts (MAS), are not substitutes for a license.
      </p>
      <p>
        Users must ensure that installation, execution,
        virtualization and redistribution of any Windows
        image comply with applicable licensing terms.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="remote-access-and-security">4. Remote access and security</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>
          The reference Windows image contains a
          passwordless user account.
        </strong>
      </p>
      <p>
        An ngrok authentication token authorizes the
        tunnel agent. It does not authenticate Remote
        Desktop clients.
      </p>
      <p>
        Compatible encrypted sessions conceal the published Remote Desktop endpoint, but public workspaces expose workflow data and older sessions or independent logs may reveal network details. Endpoint encryption is not RDP authentication, and private repositories do not, by themselves, prevent unauthorized network access.
      </p>
      <p>
        Users must configure appropriate authentication,
        network access restrictions and security updates
        before exposing a virtual machine to the internet.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="data-loss-and-persistence">5. Data loss and persistence</h2>
      <p>
        Virtual machine persistence is provided on a
        best-effort basis and depends on successful
        shutdown, disk validation and dataset uploads.
      </p>
      <p>
        Forced cancellation, infrastructure failures,
        exhausted quotas, incompatible image versions
        or interrupted uploads may result in data loss.
      </p>
      <p>
        Deleting a persistence dataset may permanently
        remove virtual machine data. Users should maintain
        independent backups of important information.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="availability-and-costs">6. Availability and costs</h2>
      <p>
        No guarantee is made regarding availability,
        performance, session duration, storage capacity
        or continued compatibility with external services.
      </p>
      <p>
        Third-party providers may modify their services,
        introduce charges, restrict usage or suspend
        accounts according to their applicable terms.
      </p>
      <p>
        Users are responsible for monitoring usage,
        quotas and any charges incurred through their
        own accounts.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="acceptable-use">7. Acceptable use</h2>
      <p>
        The project must not be used for unauthorized
        access, unlawful activity, malicious workloads,
        infringement of intellectual property rights,
        abuse of shared infrastructure or circumvention
        of provider restrictions.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="responsible-resource-usage-and-quota-abuse">8. Responsible resource usage and quota abuse</h2>
      <p>
        GitHub-hosted runners are shared computing resources.
        Free execution minutes for eligible public repositories
        must not be interpreted as unlimited computing capacity.
      </p>
      <p>
        Users must comply with GitHub Actions usage policies
        and must not use repository visibility, multiple accounts
        or automated workflows to circumvent restrictions.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Start virtual machines only for legitimate, permitted workloads.</li>
        <li>Shut down the virtual machine whenever it is no longer needed.</li>
        <li>Do not leave idle virtual machines running unnecessarily.</li>
        <li>Do not automatically restart sessions to maintain continuous uptime.</li>
        <li>Do not circumvent execution limits, quotas or account restrictions.</li>
        <li>Do not use the project for cryptocurrency mining or resource farming.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Session activity is not monitored by AuroFlow.
        The decision to start, maintain or shut down a virtual
        machine is left to the end user, who remains responsible
        for compliance with provider policies and responsible
        resource consumption.
      </p>
      <p>
        Always prefer a graceful shutdown to preserve
        your data and release runner resources.
      </p>
      <p>
        Users remain responsible for their resource consumption
        and any restrictions or account suspensions resulting
        from violations of provider policies.
      </p>
      <p>
        Refer to the
        <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/github-terms/github-terms-for-additional-products-and-features">
          GitHub Terms for Additional Products and Features
        </a>
        for the applicable GitHub Actions usage conditions.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="warranty-and-liability">9. Warranty and liability</h2>
      <p>
        The application is provided on an as-is and
        as-available basis, without warranties of
        uninterrupted operation, fitness for a particular
        purpose or absolute security.
      </p>
      <p>
        To the extent permitted by applicable law,
        the publisher shall not be liable for losses
        arising from third-party service failures,
        unauthorized use, data loss or user configuration.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nothing in this disclaimer excludes or restricts
        liability or mandatory rights that cannot
        lawfully be excluded or restricted.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="no-affiliation">10. No affiliation</h2>
      <p>
        AuroFlow is not affiliated with
        Microsoft, GitHub, Cloudflare, ngrok or Hugging Face.
        References to these services are descriptive only.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="no-managed-infrastructure-or-service-level-agreement">11. No managed infrastructure or service-level agreement</h2>
      <p>AuroFlow provides an interface for interacting with infrastructure operated by independent third parties.</p>
      <p>The publisher does not own, reserve or guarantee the availability of GitHub-hosted runners, ngrok tunnels or Hugging Face storage.</p>
      <p>No service-level agreement, guaranteed response time, guaranteed recovery time or contractual uptime commitment is provided by the project.</p>
      <p>The project should not be relied upon as the sole infrastructure for critical, emergency, production or time-sensitive operations.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="account-ownership-and-authorization">12. Account ownership and authorization</h2>
      <p>Users must have lawful access to every account, repository, dataset and service configured through the application.</p>
      <p>Users are responsible for obtaining the necessary authorization before accessing organizational resources or operating infrastructure on behalf of another person or entity.</p>
      <p>The application does not grant additional rights beyond those provided by the underlying services.</p>
      <p>Users must not submit stolen credentials, impersonate other account holders or use the project to access resources without authorization.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="oauth-permissions-and-account-security">13. OAuth permissions and account security</h2>
      <p>The GitHub OAuth authorization includes broad repository and workflow permissions.</p>
      <p>Users should review the authorization screen and understand that the resulting access token may have permissions beyond the selected instance repository.</p>
      <p>Users are responsible for protecting their accounts, access tokens, browser sessions and authentication devices.</p>
      <p>A suspected compromise should be addressed by revoking the OAuth authorization, rotating affected credentials, reviewing repository access and inspecting recent workflow activity.</p>
      <p>The application cannot guarantee protection against malicious browser extensions, compromised devices or unauthorized modifications to its source code.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="repository-visibility-and-runner-allocation">14. Repository visibility and runner allocation</h2>
      <p>Instance repositories are private by default in the standard setup.</p>
      <p>Eligible public repositories may receive different runner specifications and billing treatment from private repositories under GitHub's current policies.</p>
      <p>Public repository visibility does not authorize workloads that would otherwise violate GitHub Actions terms or acceptable use restrictions.</p>
      <p>Users must independently assess whether their intended workloads are permitted. Repository visibility must not be used as a mechanism to evade applicable restrictions.</p>
      <p>Making a repository public exposes operational logs and repository history. Encrypted sessions conceal the newly published endpoint in the control file, but older runs, logs or independent disclosures may reveal connection details. Appropriate RDP authentication and network protections remain necessary.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="end-user-discretion-and-idle-sessions">15. End-user discretion and idle sessions</h2>
      <p>The panel is not a guest activity monitoring system and does not determine whether a user is actively interacting with Windows.</p>
      <p>The decision to start, continue or gracefully shut down a virtual machine is left to the end user, within the technical limits of the workflow and the applicable provider policies.</p>
      <p>Users are expected to release shared computing resources when they are no longer required.</p>
      <p>Disconnecting an RDP client does not necessarily stop the virtual machine or the associated GitHub Actions workflow.</p>
      <p>Users should explicitly request a graceful shutdown and confirm that the workflow has completed.</p>
      <p>The absence of automatic inactivity detection does not authorize unnecessary resource consumption, continuous uptime mechanisms or quota circumvention.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="execution-limits-and-interruptions">16. Execution limits and interruptions</h2>
      <p>Virtual machine sessions are subject to workflow execution limits, runner availability, infrastructure failures and provider-imposed restrictions.</p>
      <p>The engine attempts to initiate shutdown before the workflow deadline to allow time for persistence operations.</p>
      <p>The remaining time is not a guarantee that every disk overlay can be compressed, validated and uploaded successfully.</p>
      <p>An interrupted, cancelled or timed-out workflow may terminate without saving the latest session.</p>
      <p>Users should initiate shutdown sufficiently early when preserving recent changes is important.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="persistence-integrity-and-version-compatibility">17. Persistence integrity and version compatibility</h2>
      <p>Persistent storage relies on a chain of QCOW2 disk overlays associated with a specific Golden Image checksum.</p>
      <p>The engine verifies disk and manifest integrity before restoring saved state. A checksum mismatch or incompatible backing image may prevent restoration.</p>
      <p>Integrity verification cannot establish that every file inside Windows is intact or that the guest operating system is free from logical corruption.</p>
      <p>Golden Image updates may require an explicit migration or rebase procedure. Existing overlays must not be attached to an incompatible base without appropriate validation.</p>
      <p>Firmware variables and TPM state are also relevant to virtual machine identity and may be necessary to recover encrypted or security-sensitive guest data.</p>
      <p>Users should retain independent backups and avoid treating the persistence dataset as their only recovery mechanism.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="dataset-deletion-and-destructive-operations">18. Dataset deletion and destructive operations</h2>
      <p>Deleting a persistence dataset can remove the saved virtual machine state and cause subsequent sessions to start from the reference image.</p>
      <p>Cancelling an active workflow may discard all changes made during that session.</p>
      <p>Changing repository configuration, deleting secrets or revoking access tokens may prevent the engine from starting, restoring or saving a virtual machine.</p>
      <p>Confirmation dialogs are intended to reduce accidental actions but do not eliminate the possibility of user error.</p>
      <p>Users should verify the target repository, dataset and active session before performing destructive operations.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="sensitive-information-and-regulated-workloads">19. Sensitive information and regulated workloads</h2>
      <p>The project is not represented as a certified environment for processing medical records, payment card information, classified material or other regulated or highly sensitive data.</p>
      <p>Private storage and encrypted transport do not by themselves establish compliance with industry-specific security or regulatory requirements.</p>
      <p>Users must assess their own confidentiality, encryption, access control, contractual and data protection obligations before storing sensitive information.</p>
      <p>The publisher does not provide a general compliance certification for the virtual machine infrastructure.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="guest-software-and-operating-system-maintenance">20. Guest software and operating system maintenance</h2>
      <p>The reference image is a prepared operating system installation. Its inclusion does not guarantee that every component is current, supported, secure or suitable for a particular workload.</p>
      <p>Users are responsible for installing appropriate security updates, configuring authentication and reviewing software installed inside their instances.</p>
      <p>Third-party applications, drivers and operating system components may have independent licensing, support and security requirements.</p>
      <p>The publisher does not guarantee compatibility with all Windows applications, peripherals, virtualization-dependent features or hardware acceleration requirements.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="licensing-and-distribution-responsibilities">21. Licensing and distribution responsibilities</h2>
      <p>A license covering the panel or engine source code does not grant a license to Microsoft Windows or other proprietary components.</p>
      <p>Users must verify that their own licensing arrangements permit the intended installation, virtualization and remote access scenario.</p>
      <p>The publisher remains responsible for obtaining any rights required for material distributed by the publisher.</p>
      <p>No disclaimer transfers or removes intellectual property obligations imposed directly on the publisher by applicable law.</p>
      <p>Rights holders may report concerns through contact@fox3000foxy.com.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="independent-deployments-and-modifications">22. Independent deployments and modifications</h2>
      <p>Users may operate modified or independently hosted versions of the project where permitted by the applicable source code license.</p>
      <p>Such deployments may differ from the official hosted version in their authentication flow, storage behavior, security configuration and data processing.</p>
      <p>The operator of an independent deployment is responsible for its configuration, infrastructure, privacy information and compliance obligations.</p>
      <p>The original publisher cannot guarantee the integrity or security of third-party modifications.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="support-and-maintenance-limitations">23. Support and maintenance limitations</h2>
      <p>Technical assistance, documentation and bug fixes may be provided on a voluntary basis.</p>
      <p>No commitment is made to respond to every support request, maintain every historical version or resolve issues within a particular timeframe.</p>
      <p>External API changes, infrastructure restrictions or licensing concerns may require features to be modified or removed.</p>
      <p>Users should not rely on the continued availability of the hosted authentication relay or panel as their only means of accessing or recovering their data.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="mandatory-rights-and-interpretation">24. Mandatory rights and interpretation</h2>
      <p>The provisions of this disclaimer apply only to the extent permitted by applicable law.</p>
      <p>They do not exclude liability where exclusion is prohibited, nor do they waive mandatory statutory rights or remedies.</p>
      <p>Users remain responsible for their own actions and configuration choices. The publisher remains responsible for obligations imposed directly on the publisher.</p>
      <p>If a provision is unenforceable, the remaining provisions may continue to apply to the extent permitted by law.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2 id="allocation-of-responsibilities">Allocation of responsibilities and third-party dependencies</h2>
      <p>
        AuroFlow acts as a technical interface between the end user and independently operated third-party services.
      </p>
      <p>
        The publisher does not control the internal infrastructure, operational decisions, billing practices, retention policies or contractual restrictions of these providers.
      </p>
      <p>
        Users are responsible for reviewing the applicable terms, privacy policies, licensing requirements and usage restrictions before using any integrated service.
      </p>
      <p>
        The publisher provides information and warnings concerning known technical limitations, security risks and third-party dependencies. Such information does not constitute a certification, guarantee of compliance or authorization to use external resources.
      </p>
      <p>
        Users remain responsible for their accounts, credentials, workloads, virtual machine contents, resource consumption and configuration decisions.
      </p>
      <p>
        The publisher remains responsible for its own processing activities, distribution practices and obligations under applicable law.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nothing in this provision excludes or transfers responsibilities that cannot lawfully be excluded or transferred.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
